    {
      "id": "l03-can-imperativo",
      "title": "Can, can't y el imperativo",
      "summary": "Expresar habilidad, permiso y posibilidad; dar instrucciones.",
      "minutes": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Forma",
            "Estructura",
            "Ejemplo"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Afirmativa",
              "sujeto + can + verbo base",
              "I can swim. / She can speak English."
            ],
            [
              "Negativa",
              "sujeto + cannot (can't) + verbo base",
              "He can't drive."
            ],
            [
              "Pregunta",
              "Can + sujeto + verbo base?",
              "Can you help me?"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "kind": "importante",
          "text": "**Can** es un verbo modal: es **igual para todas las personas** (no lleva s: «she can», nunca «she cans»), **no usa do / does** y va seguido del verbo **sin «to»**: «I can **go**», no «I can to go».",
          "title": "Reglas de los modales"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Uso",
            "Ejemplo",
            "Español"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Habilidad",
              "I can use AutoCAD.",
              "Sé usar AutoCAD."
            ],
            [
              "Permiso",
              "Can I come in?",
              "¿Puedo pasar?"
            ],
            [
              "Petición",
              "Can you repeat that, please?",
              "¿Puedes repetir eso?"
            ],
            [
              "Posibilidad",
              "This beam can support ten tons.",
              "Esta viga puede soportar diez toneladas."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "El imperativo"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Para dar instrucciones se usa el **verbo base, sin sujeto**. La negación se hace con **Don't**. Es la forma de los manuales, las normas y los procedimientos de laboratorio."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Inglés",
            "Español"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Open the file.",
              "Abre el archivo."
            ],
            [
              "Measure the distance twice.",
              "Mide la distancia dos veces."
            ],
            [
              "Don't touch the equipment.",
              "No toques el equipo."
            ],
            [
              "Wear a helmet at all times.",
              "Usa casco en todo momento."
            ],
            [
              "Please sit down.",
              "Por favor, siéntate."
            ],
            [
              "Let's start.",
              "Empecemos."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "Correcto:",
          "options": [
            "She cans swim",
            "She can swims",
            "She can swim",
            "She can to swim"
          ],
          "correct": 2,
          "explanation": "Can + verbo base."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "La pregunta:",
          "options": [
            "Do you can help me?",
            "Can you help me?",
            "You can help me?",
            "Can you to help me?"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Sin do."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "«No abras la puerta»:",
          "options": [
            "Not open the door",
            "Don't open the door",
            "No open the door",
            "You don't open the door"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Don't + verbo base."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "«Empecemos»:",
          "options": [
            "Let's start",
            "We start",
            "Start we",
            "Starting"
          ],
          "correct": 0,
          "explanation": "Let's + verbo."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "«Can I use your pen?» expresa:",
          "options": [
            "Habilidad",
            "Permiso",
            "Obligación",
            "Pasado"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Pedir permiso."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l04-continuous",
      "title": "Present continuous y su diferencia con el simple",
      "summary": "Lo que está pasando ahora: to be + verbo con -ing; cuándo usar cada presente.",
      "minutes": 18,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "expression": "sujeto + am / is / are + verbo-ing",
          "caption": "Present continuous"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Forma",
            "Ejemplo",
            "Español"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Afirmativa",
              "I am studying.",
              "Estoy estudiando."
            ],
            [
              "Negativa",
              "She isn't working.",
              "Ella no está trabajando."
            ],
            [
              "Pregunta",
              "Are they coming?",
              "¿Están viniendo?"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Regla del -ing",
            "Ejemplos"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "General: + ing",
              "work → working; read → reading"
            ],
            [
              "Termina en e muda: se quita la e",
              "write → writing; make → making"
            ],
            [
              "Consonante – vocal – consonante acentuada: se duplica",
              "run → running; sit → sitting; stop → stopping"
            ],
            [
              "Termina en ie: cambia a y",
              "lie → lying"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Present simple",
            "Present continuous"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Hábitos, hechos, rutinas",
              "Lo que ocurre ahora mismo o estos días"
            ],
            [
              "I work in Quito. (trabajo ahí)",
              "I am working in Quito this week. (temporal)"
            ],
            [
              "She studies every night.",
              "She is studying now."
            ],
            [
              "Señales: always, every day, usually, never",
              "Señales: now, right now, at the moment, today, this week"
            ]
          ],
          "caption": "Cuándo usar cada uno"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "kind": "importante",
          "text": "Algunos verbos **no se usan en continuous** porque expresan estados, no acciones: **know, understand, like, love, want, need, believe, have** (poseer), **be**. Se dice «I **know** the answer», no «I am knowing»; «I **want** a coffee», no «I am wanting».",
          "title": "Verbos de estado"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "kind": "nota",
          "text": "El present continuous también sirve para **planes futuros ya organizados**: «I am meeting my group tomorrow» (mañana me reúno con mi grupo)."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "Completa: «She (…) a report now.»",
          "options": [
            "writes",
            "is writing",
            "write",
            "writing"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Ahora → continuous."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "El -ing de «run» es:",
          "options": [
            "runing",
            "running",
            "runeing",
            "runs"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Se duplica la n."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "Correcto:",
          "options": [
            "I am knowing the answer",
            "I know the answer",
            "I knowing the answer",
            "I am know the answer"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Know es verbo de estado."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "«Every day» pide:",
          "options": [
            "Present continuous",
            "Present simple",
            "Imperativo",
            "Can"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Hábito."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "La pregunta:",
          "options": [
            "Do you working?",
            "Are you working?",
            "You are working?",
            "Is you working?"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "To be + sujeto + -ing."
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "El -ing de «make» es:",
          "options": [
            "makeing",
            "making",
            "makking",
            "makes"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "explanation": "Se quita la e."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l05-cantidades",
      "title": "Contables, incontables y cantidades",
      "summary": "Some, any, much, many, a lot of, a few, a little.",
      "minutes": 16,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "kind": "concepto",
          "text": "Los sustantivos **contables** se pueden contar uno por uno (one beam, two beams). Los **incontables** se ven como una masa o un concepto: water, sand, concrete, information, advice, money, time. No tienen plural ni llevan a / an.",
          "title": "Contable o incontable"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "kind": "importante",
          "text": "Palabras incontables en inglés que en español sí se cuentan: **information** (no «informations»), **advice** (consejo), **furniture** (muebles), **equipment** (equipo), **research** (investigación), **news**, **homework**. Para contarlas se usa «a piece of»: a piece of information, a piece of equipment.",
          "title": "Trampas"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Palabra",
            "Con contables",
            "Con incontables",
            "Uso"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
